    story += [Table(rows,colWidths=[30*mm,70*mm,70*mm],repeatRows=1,style=[('BACKGROUND',(0,0),(-1,0),NAVY),('TEXTCOLOR',(0,0),(-1,0),colors.white),('GRID',(0,0),(-1,-1),.5,LINE),('BACKGROUND',(0,1),(0,-1),SAND),('VALIGN',(0,0),(-1,-1),'TOP'),('LEFTPADDING',(0,0),(-1,-1),3*mm),('RIGHTPADDING',(0,0),(-1,-1),3*mm),('TOPPADDING',(0,0),(-1,-1),2.7*mm),('BOTTOMPADDING',(0,0),(-1,-1),2.7*mm)]),Spacer(1,5*mm),p('Wave-speed examples',s['h2']),p('Deep-water surface waves: &omega;<super>2</super>=gk, so c<sub>p</sub>=&omega;/k=gT/(2&pi;). For T=10 s, c<sub>p</sub>=15.61 m s<super>-1</super> and wavelength &lambda;=156.13 m. Shallow-water surface waves have c=(gH)<super>1/2</super>.',s['body']),p('Two-layer internal long waves: c=[g&prime;h<sub>1</sub>h<sub>2</sub>/(h<sub>1</sub>+h<sub>2</sub>)]<super>1/2</super>, with g&prime;=g&Delta;&rho;/&rho;<sub>0</sub>. For h<sub>1</sub>=50 m, h<sub>2</sub>=150 m, &Delta;&rho;=2 kg m<super>-3</super> and &rho;<sub>0</sub>=1025 kg m<super>-3</super>, c=0.85 m s<super>-1</super>.',s['body']),PageBreak()]
    story += [p('2. Why internal waves exist in a stratified ocean',s['h1']),p('N<super>2</super> = -(g/&rho;<sub>0</sub>) d&rho;/dz &gt; 0',s['eq']),p('Here z is positive upward. In stable stratification, density increases downward, so d&rho;/dz&lt;0 and N<super>2</super>&gt;0. If a parcel is displaced upward without immediately changing its density, it is denser than its new surroundings and sinks. If displaced downward, it is lighter and rises. Inertia carries it past equilibrium, producing an oscillation at a frequency related to the buoyancy frequency N.',s['body']),p('<b>Stability interpretation.</b> N<super>2</super>&gt;0 supports small-amplitude internal oscillations. N<super>2</super>&asymp;0 means weak restoring force in a nearly homogeneous layer. N<super>2</super>&lt;0 means gravitational instability: overturning convection grows rather than a stable small oscillation. With rotation, freely propagating internal-wave frequencies usually lie between the inertial frequency f and N.',s['body']),p('Interfacial model',s['h2']),p('The idealized ocean has two nearly homogeneous layers separated by a thin density jump. Displacement is concentrated at the interface and the layers commonly move in opposite directions. Reduced gravity summarizes the small internal density contrast. This model is useful for a sharp pycnocline and can support nonlinear solitary waves.',s['body']),p('Continuous stratification',s['h2']),p('Density and N vary smoothly with depth, so motion occupies a depth range instead of a single interface. Boundary conditions select vertical modes with nodes and phase changes; each baroclinic mode has its own speed and vertical structure. Propagating beams can also be described by the internal-wave dispersion relation. The first baroclinic mode generally has the largest vertical scale and fastest mode speed.',s['body']),img(FIG1,170),p('<b>Figure 1.</b> Locations, representative speed scales and idealized vertical modes. The comparison uses phase speed and is intended to show scale; real values depend on depth, wavelength and stratification.',s['cap']),PageBreak()]
