    story += [p('4. Submission-ready integrated answer',s['h1'])]
    answer=[
    'Ocean surface gravity waves are disturbances of the air-sea interface. Water parcels mainly move in orbital paths while the wave form and energy propagate. Gravity acting on the displaced free surface provides the restoring force. Their behavior depends on wavelength relative to water depth: deep-water waves are dispersive, with &omega;<super>2</super>=gk and phase speed c<sub>p</sub>=&omega;/k, whereas shallow-water waves travel approximately at c=(gH)<super>1/2</super>.',
    'Internal waves are oscillations of density surfaces within a stably stratified ocean. They occur because a parcel displaced vertically from equilibrium has a different density from the surrounding water. Buoyancy accelerates it back; inertia causes overshoot and sustained oscillation. With z positive upward, stable stratification has N<super>2</super>=-(g/&rho;<sub>0</sub>)d&rho;/dz&gt;0. In a rotating continuously stratified ocean, freely propagating internal waves usually satisfy f&lt;&omega;&lt;N.',
    'The two wave classes differ in at least four ways. First, surface waves occur at the free surface, while internal waves occur within the water column. Second, surface waves are restored by gravity acting across the large air-water density contrast, while internal waves are restored by buoyancy associated with much smaller seawater density differences. Third, surface gravity waves are generally faster; for example, a 10 s deep-water wave has c<sub>p</sub>&asymp;15.6 m s<super>-1</super>, whereas the example two-layer internal wave travels at about 0.85 m s<super>-1</super>. Fourth, surface elevation is commonly centimetres to several metres, whereas internal isopycnal displacement can be tens of metres or more even when its surface expression is small.',
    'In the interfacial model, two nearly homogeneous layers are separated by a sharp density jump. Displacement is concentrated at the interface, reduced gravity g&prime;=g&Delta;&rho;/&rho;<sub>0</sub> controls the restoring force, and the long-wave speed is c=[g&prime;h<sub>1</sub>h<sub>2</sub>/(h<sub>1</sub>+h<sub>2</sub>)]<super>1/2</super>. In a continuously stratified ocean, density and N vary smoothly with depth. Internal waves occupy a depth range and form vertical modes or beams; different baroclinic modes have different node patterns and propagation speeds. Thus the interfacial model is a useful simplified limit of a sharp pycnocline, while continuous stratification represents the broader vertical structure of the real ocean.' ]
